{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And Moses the servant of HaShem died there.</b> He was not called 'servant' until he died. In his lifetime Scripture called him 'the man of Elohim' at the beginning of the parashah, and now at the end of the parashah after his death it called him 'servant of HaShem' — something you will not find in all the books of the Torah. The reason is his greatness and the loftiness of his attainment, for the servant is familiar with his master and enters with him into the innermost chambers and serves before him constantly. And it is explicitly stated: 'The righteous are greater in their death than in their lifetime.' So too we find that a person is not called 'holy' until he dies, as it is written (Psalms 16:3): ״לקדושים אשר בארץ המה״, and our Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted it of the patriarchs who died, as they said in Midrash Tehillim: The Holy One, blessed be He, does not call the righteous 'holy' until they are placed in the earth, because the evil inclination presses upon a person in this world, and the Holy One, blessed be He, does not trust him until he dies — and even the patriarchs of the world were not called holy until they were placed in the earth; end quote. From this they established in prayer: ״וקדושים בכל יום יהללוך סלה״ — and it is the soul of the righteous that offers praise, as it is said (Psalms 150): ״כל הנשמה תהלל יה״. And many ask: Moses, through whom the Torah was given — how could Scripture mention death of him, when we find that one who did not reach his level, namely Enoch and Elijah, lives and exists forever? But the explanation is to say that because he sinned at the waters of Meribah he was punished with death and was included in the decree against Adam, whereas Enoch and Elijah, who did not sin, were not included in the decree against Adam, and they live eternal life." "<b>By the mouth of HaShem.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): it refers to what was said to him, ״ומות בהר״." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: it was a death by kiss — the intellect ascends to cleave to the honored Name."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:54:39.748677251Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}